    <section id="orient" class="home-section">
      <div class="orient-grid">
        <div>
          <h2 class="h-plain">What this is worth at work</h2>
          <p>Everything you have connected to so far was built for you, and bounded for you. Somebody chose which questions it would answer, capped what it returned, and decided what it would refuse. That person was doing the job you are about to do.</p>
          <p>The material is two public feeds. One publishes live vessel positions in Finnish waters; the other publishes live aircraft. Both are free, neither needs an account, and neither owes you anything — they change shape, they rate-limit, and one of them refuses you outright until you ask correctly.</p>
          <p>They also carry the problem that makes open sources worth practising on. A vessel's position comes off its transponder. Its <em>name</em>, its <em>destination</em>, its <em>type</em> and its <em>draught</em> are typed by whoever is aboard, and nothing in the protocol checks any of it. Serve those two kinds of fact in the same shape and you have destroyed the only thing that lets the person downstream weigh them.</p>
          <p>By the end you have a server a colleague can connect to in one step, whose descriptions a model reliably obeys, which cannot flood anyone's context, and which never passes off a stranger's words as your own.</p>

          <h3>What you leave with</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>A read third-party server, and a refusal you can justify from its own descriptions</li>
            <li>A written decision — tool, resource or prompt — for every capability, made before any code</li>
            <li>A working MCP server over live feeds, driven through a client before any host knows it exists</li>
            <li>A tool description you changed after watching a model choose wrongly, and watched the choice change</li>
            <li>Results that are capped, decoded, and carry what they are worth alongside what they say</li>
            <li>A handover somebody else completes on a machine you have never seen</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Before you start</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>The course repository on disk at <code>Documents\HarnessBootcamp\AI_Harness_Bootcamp</code></li>
            <li>Node and npm working — <code>node --version</code> answers in PowerShell</li>
            <li>Outbound HTTPS to the public internet. Both feeds are keyless, but they are not offline</li>
            <li><strong>GPT-5.6 Terra</strong> selected, and permissions on <strong>Ask for approval</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="orient-aside">
          <div class="lead-box">
            <p class="eyebrow">The bounds</p>
            <p>Two to five tools. One server. Local <code>stdio</code> only. Nothing that writes. No credential anywhere in the package. <strong>A surface that wants more than five tools has more than one job in it.</strong></p>
          </div>
          <div class="callout">
            <strong>You are the supplier now</strong>
            What you return lands in a context you do not own and cannot clear. Size, opacity and missing provenance are damage you do at a distance.
          </div>
          <div class="callout">
