      <section id="depth" class="home-section">
        <h2>Questions worth arguing about</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">None of these has one right answer. Each changes what you build, so have the argument before somebody has to live with your choice.</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>The specimen's author probably was not malicious. Does that change your decision?</strong> Almost certainly not — most of those patterns come from someone shipping fast, and "read the user's config for diagnostics" reads as helpful if you have never thought about who the description is talking to. It changes nothing about whether you enable it. Intent is not the control; what the words instruct the model to do is the control.</li>
          <li><strong>You capped results at twenty rows. The caller wanted thirty. Who is right?</strong> They can ask twice; nobody can un-poison a context. But hold the discomfort — you have taken a decision away from someone with more information about their own job than you have. The honest resolution is not a bigger cap, it is a result that says it was capped and how to narrow the query.</li>
          <li><strong>Is labelling everything self-reported the same as labelling nothing?</strong> A fair challenge. If every field carries a warning, the warning stops being read. The defence is that the label is structural rather than editorial — <em>observed</em> and <em>self-reported</em> are two blocks, not two adjectives, so a caller can act on the distinction in code instead of reading a caveat.</li>
          <li><strong>Should your server refuse to return a vessel name containing an instruction?</strong> Refusing hides the finding — that a vessel is broadcasting an injection is exactly what an OSINT desk exists to notice. Passing it on unmarked launders it. The third path is the one Stage 06 asks for, and it is the harder one to build.</li>
          <li><strong>This is public data about real vessels and real aircraft. Does "public" settle it?</strong> No. Aggregation changes what data is: a thousand public positions joined and queried is a capability nobody granted by switching on a transponder. It is legal, it is published for this purpose, and it is still worth knowing that you built a watching tool rather than a reading tool.</li>
          <li><strong>When is the right answer no MCP server at all?</strong> When one host needs it and nothing else will. The protocol earns its overhead when a second consumer appears — until then a direct call is faster, simpler, and has no descriptions for anyone to read.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>The same desk on other feeds</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Anything with a status field somebody types:</strong> ticket systems, asset registers, shipping manifests. The observed-versus-claimed split is the same and is almost never made.</li>
          <li><strong>Any two endpoints that will not join:</strong> a list and its details, a metric and its dimensions. Doing the join inside the surface is what turns a wrapper into a tool.</li>
          <li><strong>Any internal API you would otherwise document:</strong> the description you write for a model is a better spec than the one you would have written for a wiki, because something reads it and immediately gets it wrong in front of you.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Current primary references</h3>
        <p class="full-measure">Use current primary documentation when adapting this: <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the MCP specification</a> for primitives, transports and what the protocol does not promise; <a href="https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/extend/mcp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Codex MCP</a> for registering a local server and inspecting its surface; <a href="https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/plugins" target="_blank" rel="noopener">building plugins</a> for bundling a server into something installable; <a href="https://www.digitraffic.fi/en/marine-traffic/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Digitraffic Marine</a> and <a href="https://openskynetwork.github.io/opensky-api/rest.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the OpenSky REST API</a> for the two feeds.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="stuck" class="split-2 home-section">
        <div>
          <h2>Before you move on</h2>
          <p>The specimen was read and refused on evidence, every capability has a written primitive, the server answers live through a client, a description change moved a model's choice, results are capped and decoded and sourced, self-reported material leaves labelled, and the handover survives its own checks. Full list: <a href="#floor">these OSD lesson outcomes</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2>If you get stuck</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The marine feed returns <code>406</code>.</strong> It requires gzip and says so in the body. Send <code>Accept-Encoding: gzip</code> and a <code>Digitraffic-User</code> header identifying you. Read the error text before changing anything else — it names the fix.</li>
            <li><strong>The aircraft feed returns nothing.</strong> Your bounding box is over somewhere empty, or you have spent your budget. Check <code>x-rate-limit-remaining</code> on the response before assuming the code is wrong.</li>
            <li><strong>A vessel has no name.</strong> That is normal and it is data. The position is in one endpoint and the name in the other, and not every reporting vessel appears in both. Return the position with the name absent rather than dropping the vessel.</li>
            <li><strong>The server starts and nothing happens.</strong> That is correct. On <code>stdio</code> it is waiting to speak a protocol, not to speak to you. Drive it from a client.</li>
            <li><strong>Anything you print breaks the protocol.</strong> On <code>stdio</code>, stdout belongs to the protocol. Every log line goes to stderr, or you corrupt the channel and the failure looks like anything but a stray <code>console.log</code>.</li>
            <li><strong><code>/mcp</code> shows nothing after you edited the config.</strong> Forward slashes, absolute path, and a full quit and relaunch of the app — not just closing the window.</li>
            <li><strong>The model keeps picking the wrong tool.</strong> Two of your tools overlap. Better wording will not fix overlapping jobs; merge them, or make one refuse the other's case out loud in its description.</li>
            <li><strong>You want a sixth tool.</strong> Write down what it does that the five cannot. If the sentence contains "and", it is two tools, and probably one of them already exists.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
